            <ArticleTitle>Effect of low molecular weight heparin (fragmin) on bleeding after cardiac surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>762-4; discussion 764-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Fragmin (Dalteparin, Pharmacia Ltd, Milton Keynes, UK), a low molecular weight heparin, is now recommended in the treatment of unstable angina. Due to the greater bioavailability and longer half-life of Fragmin compared with conventional heparin we postulated that this may influence postoperative bleeding after cardiac surgery for unstable angina.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We investigated the influence of the agent on postoperative bleeding after cardiac surgery. Patients undergoing first-time coronary artery bypass grafting were prospectively studied in four groups: group A (n = 100) were elective patients; group B (n = 60) had unstable angina and received conventional heparin intravenously until operation; group C (n = 115) received Fragmin with the last dose administered more than 12 hours before surgery; and group D (n = 115) received Fragmin within 12 hours of operation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Patients in group D had significantly greater blood loss (p &lt; 0.001) and increased blood transfusion than groups A, B, and C (p = 0.047). Patients receiving Fragmin more than 12 hours before surgery (group C) had similar rates of blood loss and transfusion to group B (p &gt; 0.05) but greater than in group A (p = 0.021). There were no differences in reopening rate.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The risks of bleeding and transfusion must be weighed against the risks of acute ischemic events if Fragmin is stopped more than 12 hours before operation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
